--TEST--
`Core\IO\File::readLine` throws on a line that is not UTF-8 and leaves the handle at the line's start, and a `\r\n` whose `\r` is the last byte of one read is still one terminator — `rule:types/bytes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\IO\File::readLine
Core\IO\File $out = Core\IO::open("broken.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
$out->write("abé\nnext\n");
$out->close();

// `é` is two bytes. Writing "x" over its first byte leaves a byte that is not
// UTF-8, so the first line is no longer text.
Core\IO\File $edit = Core\IO::open("broken.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::ReadWrite);
$edit->seek(2);
$edit->write("x");
$edit->seek(0);

// The line throws twice, and the handle stays at its start both times.
uint $i = 0;
while ($i < 2) {
    try {
        $edit->readLine();
        echo "not reached\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        echo "not text: ", $e->message, "\n";
    }
    echo "still at ", $edit->tell() as string, "\n";
    $i = $i + 1;
}

// A program can skip the line itself and read on.
$edit->seek(5);
echo "then: [", $edit->readLine() ?? "<end>", "]\n";
$edit->close();

// `readLine` reads 8192 bytes at a time. Here the `\r` of a `\r\n` is byte 8192,
// so the `\n` arrives in the next read. It is one terminator, and the handle
// stands after both bytes.
Core\IO\File $wide = Core\IO::open("edge.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Write);
$wide->write(Core\Str::repeat("x", 8191));
$wide->write("\r\nz\r");
$wide->close();
Core\IO\File $walk = Core\IO::open("edge.txt", Core\IO\FileMode::Read);
echo "long: ", Core\Str::length($walk->readLine() ?? "") as string, "\n";
echo "at ", $walk->tell() as string, "\n";
echo "next: [", $walk->readLine() ?? "<end>", "]\n";
echo "last: [", $walk->readLine() ?? "<end>", "]\n";
$walk->close();
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
not text: Core\IO\File::readLine: what was read is not valid UTF-8, so it is not a `string` — byte 3 is where it stops being text
still at 0
not text: Core\IO\File::readLine: what was read is not valid UTF-8, so it is not a `string` — byte 3 is where it stops being text
still at 0
then: [next]
long: 8191
at 8193
next: [z]
last: [<end>]
